window.docContents[153].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-the-2d-Lie-algebra_SEMI_-Lie-theorem-in-positive-characteristic/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras--Irreducible-representations-of-the-2d-Lie-algebra_SEMI_-Lie-theorem-in-positive-characteristic--Irreducible-representations-of-the-2d-Lie-algebra_SEMI_-Lie-theorem-in-positive-characteristic--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A type-valued construction determined by the displayed parameters.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nA type-valued construction determined by the displayed parameters.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe bracket of the displayed elements has the stated value.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples_continued/Derived3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nIn characteristic zero, there is a unique scalar parameter for an equivalence with the displayed one-dimensional module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe finite rank of the displayed module has the stated value.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nEvery finite-dimensional irreducible module is equivalent to either a one-parameter module or a module in the displayed modular family.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe displayed classification of finite-dimensional irreducible modules has the stated uniqueness of parameters.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nA Lie subalgebra of two-by-two matrices over the displayed field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe displayed matrix Lie algebra is solvable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nEach module in the displayed modular family is irreducible.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nTwo modules in the modular family are equivalent exactly when their parameters satisfy the displayed conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\nA displayed one-dimensional module is not equivalent to a module in the modular family.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.16. Problems on Lie algebras\u0009Irreducible representations of the 2d Lie algebra; Lie theorem in positive characteristic\u0009Irreducible representations of the 2d Lie algebra; Lie theorem in positive characteristic\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-the-2d-Lie-algebra_SEMI_-Lie-theorem-in-positive-characteristic/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras--Irreducible-representations-of-the-2d-Lie-algebra_SEMI_-Lie-theorem-in-positive-characteristic--Irreducible-representations-of-the-2d-Lie-algebra_SEMI_-Lie-theorem-in-positive-characteristic--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___4___-Filtrations--Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients--Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"There exists auxiliary data whose associated relation series has simple displayed successive\nquotients.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Introduction_to_3.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Lemma3.4.2; role=supporting]\n\nA finite-dimensional module admits a relation series with bottom head and top last term.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Lemma3.4.2/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.7.1/Derived17; role=supporting]\n\nDeclaration: Submodule.Quotient.module\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Lemma3.4.2/Derived5; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: Submodule.comap\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Lemma3.4.2/Derived7; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: exists_compositionSeries_of_isNoetherian_isArtinian\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Lemma3.4.2/Derived4; role=supporting\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Lemma3.4.2/Derived6; role=supporting\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.4. Filtrations\u0009Existence of filtration with irreducible successive quotients\u0009Existence of filtration with irreducible successive quotients\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___4___-Filtrations--Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients--Existence-of-filtration-with-irreducible-successive-quotients--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Unitary-representations-are-completely-reducible/#Chapter4___Theorem4___6___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.6. Unitary representations. Another proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations\u0009Unitary representations are completely reducible\u0009Unitary representations are completely reducible","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Unitary-representations-are-completely-reducible/#Chapter4___Theorem4___6___3___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Derivation-of-dimension-formula-for-V_lambda-and-definition-of-hook/#Chapter5___Discussion_hook_length_derivation":{"contents":"Let us use the Frobenius character formula to compute the dimension of V_\\lambda. According to the character formula, \\dim V_\\lambda is the coefficient of x^{\\lambda + \\rho} in \\Delta(x)(x_1 + \\cdots + x_N)^n. Let l_j = \\lambda_j + N - j. Then, using the determinant formula for \\Delta(x) and expanding the determinant as a sum over permutations, we get\n\n\n\\dim V_\\lambda = \\sum_{s \\in S_N : l_j \\geq N - s(j)} (-1)^s \\frac{n!}{\\prod_j (l_j - N + s(j))!}\n\n\n\n= \\frac{n!}{l_1! \\ldots l_N!} \\sum_{s \\in S_N} (-1)^s \\prod_j l_j(l_j - 1) \\ldots (l_j - N + s(j) + 1)\n\n\n\n= \\frac{n!}{\\prod_j l_j!} \\det(l_j(l_j - 1) \\ldots (l_j - N + i + 1)).\n\n\nUsing column reduction and the Vandermonde determinant formula, we see from this expression that\n\n\n(5.17.1) \\qquad \\dim V_\\lambda = \\frac{n!}{\\prod_j l_j!} \\det(l_j^{N-i}) = \\frac{n!}{\\prod_j l_j!} \\prod_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq N} (l_i - l_j)\n\n\n(where N \\geq p).\n\nIn this formula, there are many cancellations. After making some of these cancellations, we obtain the hook length formula. Namely, for a square (i, j) in a Young diagram \\lambda (i, j \\geq 1, i \\leq \\lambda_j), define the\nhook of (i, j) to be the set of all squares (i', j') in \\lambda with i' \\geq i, j' = j or i' = i, j' \\geq j. Let h(i, j) be the length of the hook of i, j, i.e., the number of squares in it.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.17. The hook length formula\u0009Derivation of dimension formula for V_lambda and definition of hook","header":"Derivation of dimension formula for V_lambda and definition of hook","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Derivation-of-dimension-formula-for-V_lambda-and-definition-of-hook/#Chapter5___Discussion_hook_length_derivation"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Q-span-equivalent-to-C-span-in-Artin___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Remark5___26___2":{"contents":"Remark 5.26.2. Statement (ii) of Theorem 5.26.1 is equivalent to the same statement with \\mathbb{Q}-span replaced by \\mathbb{C}-span. Indeed, consider the matrix whose columns consist of the coefficients of the decomposition of \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G V (for various H, V) with respect to the irreducible representations of G. Then both statements are equivalent to the condition that the rows of this matrix are linearly independent.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.26. Artin's theorem\u0009Q-span equivalent to C-span in Artin's theorem","header":"Q-span equivalent to C-span in Artin's theorem","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Q-span-equivalent-to-C-span-in-Artin___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Remark5___26___2"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___--Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2--Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The three-element indexed family of standard linear-map representations.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.2.3; role=supporting]\n\nEvery member of the three-element family of standard models is indecomposable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.2.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn indecomposable representation has dimension pair (1, 0), (0, 1), or (1, 1), with an injective structure map in the last case.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Example6.2.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$\u0009Example 6.2.3: Indecomposable representations of A2\u0009Example 6.2.3: Indecomposable representations of A2\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___--Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2--Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots","header":"Lemma 6.4.2: B is positive definite and even-valued","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Remark-6___4___4___-Finiteness-of-roots/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Remark-6___4___4___-Finiteness-of-roots--Remark-6___4___4___-Finiteness-of-roots--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Under the given condition on the integer matrix, the set of integer-valued vectors satisfying the predicate is finite.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Remark6.4.4; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots\u0009Remark 6.4.4: Finiteness of roots\u0009Remark 6.4.4: Finiteness of roots\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Remark-6___4___4___-Finiteness-of-roots/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Remark-6___4___4___-Finiteness-of-roots--Remark-6___4___4___-Finiteness-of-roots--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Representable-functors-for-enriched-categories/#Chapter7___Remark7___5___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.5. Representable functors\u0009Representable functors for enriched categories\u0009Representable functors for enriched categories","header":"Formalization","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Representable-functors-for-enriched-categories/#Chapter7___Remark7___5___2___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Exact-sequences-of-vector-spaces-split/#Chapter7___Exercise7___8___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology\u0009Exact sequences of vector spaces split\u0009Exact sequences of vector spaces split","header":"Formalization","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Exact-sequences-of-vector-spaces-split/#Chapter7___Exercise7___8___4___formalization"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Flat-modules/#Chapter8___Problem8___1___3":{"contents":"Problem 8.1.3. A right A-module M is said to be flat if the functor M \\otimes_A on the category of left A-modules is exact.\n\n(i) Show that any projective module is flat.\n\n(ii) Let A be a commutative ring and let S be any multiplicatively closed subset of A. Then, the localization S^{-1}A is a flat A-module.\n\n(iii) Let A = \\mathbb{C}[x], M = \\mathbb{C}[x, x^{-1}]. Show that M is flat but not projective.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Flat modules","header":"Flat modules","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Flat-modules/#Chapter8___Problem8___1___3"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.1. Lifting of idempotents","header":"Complete system of orthogonal idempotents","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents"}});